  "note": "THE SEA CONDITIONS (0215, docs/SEA_CONDITIONS_AND_REGIONS.md §2). Every named sea of data/seas.json is a zone, and the oceans are cut into zones by lat/lon boxes laid over the sea raster (0040). A zone asks a TITLE (public.title_level: the lower of the house's Trading and Exploration levels) and a WAVE resistance (public.fleet_wave: the fleet's average world.ship_stat 'wave'). `zones` are in REQUIREMENT ORDER (public.sea_refusal names the first zone that refuses in this order; the array index + 1 is sea_zones.ord). Each zone's `boxes` name the raster sea (`sea` = seas.code) and the half-open bounds [min, max) in degrees; a bound left out is unbounded; a box with no bounds is THE REST of its sea. Within one sea the boxes are tried in `ord` order (sea_zone_boxes.ord, unique per sea), lowest first, so a sea's rest box carries its highest ord; every sea has exactly one rest box, so every water cell answers one zone (asserted by 0215 and by scripts/db/world-guard.mjs). `titles` are the eleven title names (public.title_names), level 1 to title_max. The world guard holds public.sea_zones, public.sea_zone_boxes and public.title_names EQUAL to this file, both directions. The numbers are the design's (§2, §7 Q7: every one accepted by the owner, 2026-10-20).",
